usly  um important that we included  asymptomatic transmission so  transmission from people who didn't have  symptoms  and so  uh  what you try and do with your models is  include as much data as possible  and where you don't have data you try  and be as agnostic as possible so you  don't assign  a probability of  associated with that particular thing  there's an infinite amount of modeling  that one could do for something like  covid so how do you decide how to narrow  that down and who ultimately is making  the call about what you are going to  model  well  so that's really um  a mixture of different a different a  mixture of different processes  um so there's some policy ask so  there's some decision making that needs  to be done then the  sage and the spam secretariat they  transform policy asks into a question  that can be modeled that can be answered  with modelling  um  and so there's that's one route another  route is  can you say sorry just to clarify  when you say sorry just to clarify when  you say policy asks you mean officials  in whitehall want certain questions  answered and they put them to spy m and  the sage groups yes  and then uh the other route is  through working with clinicians and  working with public health officials and  um  just  kind of the other general  general roots through the scientific  discourse other questions come up and  we work on those because they because we  consider them important or somebody  considers them important so  it's a combination of  policy asks and big scientific questions  which are communicated within the  scientific community in general  does sage sage acts independently from  government and yet there's as we've just  been discussing a lot of overlap between  the requests and what's being returned  does sage ever get requests about being  asked to input something or model  something and actually think to  themselves  that's not the right question to be  asking that doesn't quite add up that's  too optimistic 